.TH ORA-CHAR 1 "November 2026" "ora-char 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ora-char \- convert Oracle internal CHAR and VARCHAR2 bytes into characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ora-char
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR format ]
.I bytes
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ora-char
decodes an Oracle
.B CHAR
or
.B VARCHAR2
value from its internal byte representation, as shown by
.BR DUMP() ,
.BR "DUMP(x, 16)" ,
.B RAWTOHEX()
or a block dump, and prints its characters.
.PP
This release supports ASCII data only; bytes of 0x80 and above are rejected.
.B NCHAR
and
.B NVARCHAR2
are not supported (see
.BR "INPUT FORMATS" ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIformat\fR"
The input format.
.I format
is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B auto
Detect the input format (see
.BR "INPUT FORMATS" ).
This is the default.
.TP
.B dec
Decimal bytes, e.g.\&
.BR 97,98,99 .
.TP
.B hex
Hex bytes, e.g.\&
.BR 616263 ,
.B 61,62,63
or
.BR "col 0: [ 3] 61 62 63" .
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the characters unchanged instead of escaping control characters and
backslashes (see
.BR OUTPUT ).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also print the type and character set (if given in a
.B DUMP()
header), the format the input was read in, the length and the bytes.
The value is shown in double quotes so that the blank padding of
.B CHAR
values is visible.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH INPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B DUMP() output
.B "Typ=1 Len=3: 97,98,99"
(decimal) or
.B "Typ=1 Len=3: 61,62,63"
.RB ( "DUMP(x, 16)" ,
hex).
The type must be 1
.RB ( VARCHAR2 )
or 96
.RB ( CHAR ),
and the number of bytes must match
.BR Len .
A
.B CharacterSet=
field, as printed by
.B "DUMP(x, 1010)"
and
.BR "DUMP(x, 1016)" ,
is accepted.
.IP
.B DUMP()
shows
.B NCHAR
and
.B NVARCHAR2
values with the same type codes, so
.B ora-char
rejects data in their usual national character set,
.BR AL16UTF16 :
an even number of bytes in which every other byte, starting with the first,
is zero, or a
.B CharacterSet=AL16UTF16
field.
With the
.B UTF8
national character set, ASCII text has the same bytes as in
.B CHAR
and
.B VARCHAR2
and cannot be detected.
.IP
The input
.B NULL
is reported as an error.
.TP
.B Byte list
Bytes separated by commas or whitespace, e.g.\&
.B 97,98,99
or
.BR "61 62 63" .
In hex, a token may hold several bytes and an optional
.B 0x
prefix, e.g.\&
.B "6162 63"
or
.BR 0x61,0x62 .
.TP
.B Contiguous hex
As returned by
.BR RAWTOHEX() ,
with an optional
.B 0x
prefix, e.g.\&
.BR 0x616263 .
.TP
.B Block dump
A column entry such as
.BR "col 0: [ 3] 61 62 63" .
The bytes are always hex, and their number must match the count in brackets;
.B "\-f dec"
is rejected for this format.
A NULL column
.RB ( "col 1: *NULL*" )
is reported as an error.
.PP
With
.BR "\-f auto" ,
block dump input is read as hex.
Other input is read as hex if it is a single contiguous token, contains hex
letters, or consists of space-separated two-character pairs; otherwise it is
read as decimal.
If only the other format gives printable text of the declared length, the
other format is used instead.
If both formats give printable text, the detected format is used and a note
on standard error shows the other reading; use
.B \-f
to choose.
.SH OUTPUT
Printable ASCII characters are printed as they are.
Backslashes and control characters are escaped as
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \er ,
.B \e0
and
.BI \ex NN
for all other control characters, including 0x7F.
With
.BR \-\-raw ,
the bytes are printed unchanged.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if the input cannot be parsed or is not ASCII, and 2 for
invalid command line options.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ ora-char "Typ=1 Len=5: 72,101,108,108,111"
Hello
.sp
$ ora-char 48656C6C6F
Hello
.sp
$ ora-char "col 0: [ 5] 48 65 6c 6c 6f"
Hello
.sp
$ ora-char \-f hex "Typ=1 Len=3: 61,62,63"
abc
.sp
$ ora-char \-v "Typ=96 Len=5: 104,105,32,32,32"
type:     CHAR (Typ=96)
format:   dec
length:   5
bytes:    68 69 20 20 20
value:    "hi   "
.sp
$ ora-char \-v "Typ=1 Len=5 CharacterSet=AL32UTF8: 48,65,6c,6c,6f"
type:     VARCHAR2 (Typ=1)
charset:  AL32UTF8
format:   hex
length:   5
bytes:    48 65 6C 6C 6F
value:    "Hello"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ora-num (1)
